menu "ESP Remote ID"

config REMOTEID_TRANSPORT_BLE
    bool "Enable Bluetooth LE legacy advertisements"
    default y
    help
        Broadcast OpenDroneID messages using BLE legacy advertisements with
        service UUID 0xFFFA. Disable this when testing Wi-Fi-only reception.

config REMOTEID_TRANSPORT_BLE5_LONG_RANGE
    bool "Enable Bluetooth 5 Long Range (LE Coded PHY)"
    default n
    help
        Broadcast OpenDroneID Message Packs (all messages in one
        advertisement) using BLE 5 extended advertising with LE Coded PHY (S=8
        coding). This increases range at the cost of a lower
        data rate and is a separate transport from BLE 4 legacy advertising.
        Both can be enabled simultaneously.

        Disabled by default. EU regulation 2019/945 does not list transports;
        it requires a broadcast that "can be received directly by existing
        mobile devices". Most phones cannot receive LE Coded PHY, so never use
        BLE 5 Long Range on its own in the EU: keep BLE 4 legacy (or Wi-Fi)
        enabled alongside it.

        Most current receiver apps do not yet support BLE 5 Long Range. Enable
        alongside BLE 4 for extended range, or alone when operating under FAA
        rules and targeting F3411-22a compatible receivers.

config REMOTEID_TRANSPORT_WIFI_BEACON
    bool "Enable Wi-Fi Beacon advertisements"
    default n
    help
        Broadcast OpenDroneID Message Pack payloads in Wi-Fi Beacon vendor
        information elements. This can be enabled together with BLE or used
        alone when testing receiver transport support.

config REMOTEID_TRANSPORT_WIFI_NAN
    bool "Enable Wi-Fi NAN advertisements"
    default n
    help
        Broadcast OpenDroneID Message Pack payloads in Wi-Fi Neighbor
        Awareness Networking sync/action frames. This is a separate transport
        from Wi-Fi Beacon and receiver apps may support one but not the other.

choice REMOTEID_IDENTITY_SOURCE
    prompt "Remote ID identity source"
    default REMOTEID_IDENTITY_STATIC
    help
        Where the broadcast identity (UAS ID, Operator ID, ID type, UA type,
        EU operation category and equipment class) comes from.

config REMOTEID_IDENTITY_STATIC
    bool "Static (configured here, flashed in)"
    help
        Identity is taken from the options below at build time. Use this for a
        standalone module on a fixed drone.

config REMOTEID_IDENTITY_RUNTIME
    bool "Runtime (provided over MAVLink / DroneCAN)"
    help
        Identity is left empty and undeclared at boot and supplied at runtime by
        a flight controller or GCS. The module stays not-ready (no broadcast)
        until a valid Basic ID and Operator ID arrive. The static identity
        options below are hidden in this mode, since they would only be
        overwritten by the incoming messages. In strict compliance mode the
        UAS ID still comes from eFuse and the UA type from the option below;
        only the Operator ID (and System data) come from the flight controller
        or GCS.

endchoice

config REMOTEID_UAS_ID
    string "UAS ID (max 20 characters)"
    depends on REMOTEID_IDENTITY_STATIC && !REMOTEID_COMPLIANCE_STRICT
    default "CHANGE_ME_UAS_ID"
    help
        The UAS ID broadcast in the Basic ID message. In the EU this is the
        ANSI/CTA-2063-A serial number of the Remote ID system: for an add-on
        module its own serial, for a built-in system the serial of the
        aircraft. It has the form <4-character manufacturer code><length
        character 1-F><serial>, using digits and upper-case letters without O
        and I, e.g. "1ABC5XYZ12".

        A CAA registration number can be used instead (ID type "CAA
        Registration ID"). Must be 20 characters or fewer. Not used in strict
        compliance mode, which reads the serial from eFuse.

config REMOTEID_OPERATOR_ID
    string "Operator registration ID (max 20 characters)"
    depends on REMOTEID_IDENTITY_STATIC
    default "CHANGE_ME_OP_ID"
    help
        Your national operator registration number as issued by your Civil
        Aviation Authority. For an EU number enter it with its 3 secret
        characters, e.g. "FIN87astrdge12k8-xyz": the checksum is verified
        against the secret part and only the 16 public characters are
        broadcast. Strict compliance mode accepts only this form.

config REMOTEID_COMPLIANCE_STRICT
    bool "Strict EU (EASA) compliance mode"
    depends on REMOTEID_MAVLINK_INPUT || REMOTEID_DRONECAN_INPUT
    default n
    help
        Enforce the EU direct remote identification rules (Regulation (EU)
        2019/945, Part 6 of the Annex) at runtime. Needs a live position
        source (MAVLink or DroneCAN input).

        - The UAS ID is the ANSI/CTA-2063-A serial number burned into the
          eFuse user data block (BLOCK_USR_DATA) at production, so neither
          input links nor a reflash can change it. Basic ID messages from
          MAVLink or DroneCAN are ignored. A malformed serial is never
          broadcast: the device stays not-ready, shows the error indicator and
          reports the failure to the autopilot.
        - The operator ID must be the full EU registration number with its 3
          secret characters. The checksum is verified and only the 16 public
          characters are stored and broadcast. A failed check is reported to
          the operator until a valid number is uploaded.
        - The aircraft Location is taken only from the bound autopilot (MAVLink)
          or the bound node (DroneCAN), never from a ground station, and the
          device is only ready to arm with a fresh Location that has a
          position, a height and a time stamp.
        - No fixed configured position, no start-up delay, and the OTA server
          only with Secure Boot (signed images).
        - At least one EN 4709-002 broadcast method is enabled: BLE 5 Long
          Range, Wi-Fi Beacon or Wi-Fi NAN. Keep BLE 4 legacy advertising on
          as well, since phones without those receivers rely on it.

        A production device also needs Secure Boot and flash encryption, so
        the position handling and the rest of the firmware cannot be changed
        by reflashing.

        When disabled, a malformed serial is logged as a warning but still
        broadcast, and a 16-character operator ID is accepted unverified
        (development convenience).

config REMOTEID_STARTUP_DELAY_MS
    int "Startup delay before transmissions (ms)"
    depends on !REMOTEID_COMPLIANCE_STRICT
    default 10000
    range 0 60000
    help
        Delay after boot before BLE or Wi-Fi Remote ID transmissions start.
        This is useful during development because it leaves time to attach the
        serial monitor after flashing or reset.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_INPUT
    bool "Enable MAVLink OpenDroneID input"
    default n
    help
        Parse incoming MAVLink v2 OpenDroneID messages from a flight controller
        or companion computer and feed them into the Remote ID state store.
        Supported messages are OPEN_DRONE_ID_BASIC_ID, OPEN_DRONE_ID_OPERATOR_ID,
        OPEN_DRONE_ID_LOCATION, OPEN_DRONE_ID_SYSTEM, OPEN_DRONE_ID_SELF_ID,
        OPEN_DRONE_ID_AUTHENTICATION, OPEN_DRONE_ID_MESSAGE_PACK and
        OPEN_DRONE_ID_SYSTEM_UPDATE, plus HEARTBEAT and SYSTEM_TIME from the
        autopilot (arm state, takeoff capture, UTC clock). Messages that
        describe another aircraft (id_or_mac set, or sent by an Open Drone ID
        transmitter/receiver component) are ignored.

if REMOTEID_MAVLINK_INPUT

choice REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TRANSPORT
    prompt "MAVLink transport"
    default REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TRANSPORT_UART
    help
        Physical link used to exchange MAVLink with the flight controller or
        companion computer.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TRANSPORT_UART
    bool "UART"
    help
        Hardware UART. Requires wiring the RX (and optionally TX) GPIOs to the
        peer's serial port.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TRANSPORT_USB_SERIAL_JTAG
    bool "USB Serial/JTAG (native USB device)"
    help
        Use the ESP32-S3 built-in USB Serial/JTAG peripheral on the native USB
        port (GPIO19/20) as a USB CDC device, exchanging MAVLink with a USB host
        such as a companion computer or SBC. No GPIO wiring is needed. Set the
        console secondary output to "None" so log text does not corrupt the
        MAVLink byte stream; the build will fail with a clear message otherwise.

endchoice

if REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TRANSPORT_UART

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_UART_NUM
    int "MAVLink UART number"
    default 1
    range 0 2
    help
        ESP32-S3 UART peripheral used for MAVLink input. This selects the
        hardware UART block, not the physical pins. The ESP32-S3 GPIO matrix
        lets UART signals be routed to many different pins, so configure the RX
        and optional TX GPIOs below to match your board wiring.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_BAUD
    int "MAVLink UART baud rate"
    default 57600
    range 1200 921600
    help
        Baud rate for the MAVLink input UART. Common flight-controller values
        are 57600, 115200, and 921600.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_RX_GPIO
    int "MAVLink UART RX GPIO"
    default -1
    range -1 48
    help
        Physical ESP32-S3 GPIO used as the selected UART's RX signal. Connect
        this pin to the flight controller or companion computer MAVLink TX pin.
        This must be set to a valid GPIO when MAVLink input is enabled. Do not
        assume the UART number automatically selects a usable board pin.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TX_GPIO
    int "MAVLink UART TX GPIO"
    default -1
    range -1 48
    help
        Optional physical ESP32-S3 GPIO used as the selected UART's TX signal.
        Connect this pin to the flight controller or companion computer MAVLink
        RX pin for bidirectional wiring. Receive-only OpenDroneID input does not
        need it, but it is required when status output (HEARTBEAT and
        OPEN_DRONE_ID_ARM_STATUS) is enabled below. Leave at -1 for RX only.

endif # REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TRANSPORT_UART

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TASK_STACK
    int "MAVLink input task stack size"
    default 4096
    range 3072 8192

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TARGET_SYSTEM
    int "Accepted MAVLink target system"
    default 0
    range 0 255
    help
        If non-zero, only OpenDroneID messages addressed to this target system
        or broadcast target 0 are accepted. Leave as 0 to accept all systems.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_TARGET_COMPONENT
    int "Accepted MAVLink target component"
    default 0
    range 0 255
    help
        If non-zero, only OpenDroneID messages addressed to this target
        component or broadcast target 0 are accepted. Leave as 0 to accept all
        components.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_EMERGENCY_FROM_HEARTBEAT
    bool "Derive ODID emergency status from autopilot HEARTBEAT"
    default n
    help
        Map the flight controller's HEARTBEAT system_status to the OpenDroneID
        operational status: MAV_STATE_EMERGENCY and MAV_STATE_FLIGHT_TERMINATION
        are broadcast as EMERGENCY. Useful when the LOCATION source does not set
        the ODID status itself. By default the emergency latches until a power
        cycle (see the latch option below). A latched self-test system-failure
        keeps precedence.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_EMERGENCY_INCLUDE_CRITICAL
    bool "Also treat MAV_STATE_CRITICAL (failsafe) as emergency"
    depends on REMOTEID_MAVLINK_EMERGENCY_FROM_HEARTBEAT
    default n
    help
        Include CRITICAL (e.g. RC or battery failsafe) as an OpenDroneID
        emergency. Off by default because routine failsafes such as a battery RTL
        set CRITICAL, which may be too aggressive to broadcast as an emergency.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_EMERGENCY_LATCH
    bool "Latch emergency status (clear only by power cycle)"
    depends on REMOTEID_MAVLINK_EMERGENCY_FROM_HEARTBEAT
    default y
    help
        Once an emergency is declared, keep broadcasting EMERGENCY until a power
        cycle; do not downgrade it automatically when the autopilot recovers.
        This is the safe, conservative behaviour (an emergency should not be
        silently erased) and is the default. Disable only for live tracking that
        follows the autopilot state up and down.

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_STATUS_OUTPUT
    bool "Send HEARTBEAT and OPEN_DRONE_ID_ARM_STATUS to the flight controller"
    default n
    help
        Periodically transmit a MAVLink HEARTBEAT (component type Open Drone ID)
        and an OPEN_DRONE_ID_ARM_STATUS message back to the flight controller.
        ArduPilot uses these to detect that a Remote ID module is present and
        healthy, and (depending on DID_OPTIONS) can refuse to arm without them.
        Requires the MAVLink UART TX GPIO above to be set.

# Outgoing identity is shared by every MAVLink producer (status output and the
# operator-location loop), so it is available whenever either is enabled.
if REMOTEID_MAVLINK_STATUS_OUTPUT || REMOTEID_MAVLINK_OPERATOR_LOCATION_LOOP

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_OWN_SYSTEM_ID
    int "Outgoing MAVLink system ID"
    default 0
    range 0 255
    help
        MAVLink system ID used for messages this module sends (HEARTBEAT,
        OPEN_DRONE_ID_ARM_STATUS, OPEN_DRONE_ID_SYSTEM_UPDATE). A Remote ID
        transmitter should share the flight controller's system ID. Leave at 0 to
        adopt the system ID seen in the flight controller's HEARTBEAT
        automatically (system ID 1 is used until one is observed).

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_OWN_COMPONENT_ID
    int "Outgoing MAVLink component ID"
    default 236
    range 1 255
    help
        MAVLink component ID used for outgoing messages. 236, 237, and 238 are
        the Open Drone ID transmitter/receiver component IDs
        (MAV_COMP_ID_ODID_TXRX_1..3). Use a distinct value per module if several
        share one system ID.

endif # outgoing identity

if REMOTEID_MAVLINK_STATUS_OUTPUT

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_STATUS_RATE_HZ
    int "Status output rate (Hz)"
    default 1
    range 1 10
    help
        How often HEARTBEAT and OPEN_DRONE_ID_ARM_STATUS are transmitted. 1 Hz
        matches the usual MAVLink heartbeat cadence and is sufficient for
        ArduPilot's arm gating.

endif # REMOTEID_MAVLINK_STATUS_OUTPUT

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_OPERATOR_LOCATION_LOOP
    bool "Own the operator-location freshness loop (SYSTEM_UPDATE)"
    default n
    help
        Keep the OpenDroneID operator location fresh on the aircraft without the
        GCS staying connected. For a fixed operator location (FIXED/TAKEOFF) the
        firmware periodically transmits OPEN_DRONE_ID_SYSTEM_UPDATE (broadcast
        target 0/0) to the autopilot. A live (LIVE_GNSS) operator location is
        never republished; it expires as set by "Live operator-location expiry".
        The send timestamp is seeded from MAVLink SYSTEM_TIME. Requires an outbound MAVLink
        path: the TX GPIO on the UART transport, or USB Serial/JTAG (inherent).
        Operator-location status is reported via ARM_STATUS only when status
        output is also enabled.

if REMOTEID_MAVLINK_OPERATOR_LOCATION_LOOP

config REMOTEID_MAVLINK_OPERATOR_LOCATION_PERIOD_MS
    int "Fixed operator-location republish period (ms)"
    default 2000
    range 200 10000
    help
        Interval between OPEN_DRONE_ID_SYSTEM_UPDATE transmissions when a fixed
        operator location is active. 2000 ms (0.5 Hz) mirrors a typical GCS.

endif # REMOTEID_MAVLINK_OPERATOR_LOCATION_LOOP

endif # REMOTEID_MAVLINK_INPUT

config REMOTEID_OPERATOR_LOCATION_EXPIRY_MS
    int "Live operator-location expiry (ms)"
    depends on REMOTEID_MAVLINK_INPUT || REMOTEID_DRONECAN_INPUT
    default 10000
    range 1000 60000
    help
        A live (LIVE_GNSS) operator location whose source timestamp has not
        advanced within this window is stale. It is then replaced by the
        take-off point captured at arming (EU 2019/945: the position of the
        remote pilot or, if not available, the take-off point), or dropped
        when there is none. Only a newer source timestamp restores it.

config REMOTEID_DRONECAN_INPUT
    bool "Enable DroneCAN (ArduPilot OpenDroneID over CAN)"
    default n
    help
        Join the CAN bus as a DroneCAN node and take the Remote ID data from
        ArduPilot's OpenDroneID CAN backend (DID_CANDRIVER):

        - receives dronecan.remoteid.BasicID, Location, SelfID, System and
          OperatorID from the first node that sends them (the autopilot);
        - sends dronecan.remoteid.ArmStatus and uavcan.protocol.NodeStatus at
          1 Hz and answers uavcan.protocol.GetNodeInfo.

        On ArduPilot set DID_ENABLE=1, DID_CANDRIVER to the CAN driver of this
        bus (1 or 2) and DID_MAVPORT=-1. DroneCAN has no Authentication message.

if REMOTEID_DRONECAN_INPUT

